.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KSNAP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ksnap
.Nd take, list, roll back and destroy ZFS snapshots by path
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Ar path
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm rollback
.Ar path
.Nm
.Cm destroy
.Ar dataset Ns @ Ns Ar name
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a short way to the ZFS snapshots of a kldload machine's own
filesystems, named by the directory they hold rather than by dataset.
A snapshot takes no time and no space when taken, and holds exactly what
the filesystem held at that moment.
.Pp
A
.Ar path
is resolved to the ZFS dataset mounted there or above it:
.Pa /home/admin
is
.Ql rpool/home/admin
if that is a dataset of its own, and
.Ql rpool/home
if not.
A path on anything but ZFS is refused.
.Pp
Snapshots taken by
.Nm
are named
.Ql manual- Ns Ar YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS ,
which sets them apart from the ones taken on a schedule.
.Pp
.Nm
calls
.Xr zfs 8
through
.Xr sudo 8 ;
run as a user it may ask for a password.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It (no argument)
Snapshot the datasets that hold the machine's own state: the boot
environments under
.Pa rpool/ROOT ,
the home directories under
.Pa rpool/home ,
.Pa rpool/srv
and
.Pa rpool/var/lib ,
those that exist, all with the same name.
Each is reported; any that fails is named, and the exit status is 1.
.It Ar path
Snapshot the dataset holding
.Ar path .
.It Cm list
Every snapshot on the machine, newest first, with its creation time and
the space it holds on its own, and the total.
.It Cm rollback Ar path
Return the dataset holding
.Ar path
to its newest snapshot, whoever took it.
Everything written since is gone.
The running root filesystem is refused: going back there is done by
booting an earlier boot environment, with
.Xr kldload-rollback 8 .
.It Cm destroy Ar dataset Ns @ Ns Ar name
Destroy one snapshot.
A name without
.Ql @
is refused, since it would name the dataset itself.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
A snapshot, rollback or destroy failed, a path is not on ZFS, or the
dataset has no snapshot to roll back to.
.It 2
A usage error: an unknown command, a missing operand, or a dataset given
to
.Cm destroy .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-29.
.Ql rpool/ksnaptest-3296323 ,
mounted at
.Pa /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323 ,
was made for the purpose and destroyed afterwards, as were the snapshots
the first example took.
.Ss The machine's own datasets (no argument)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap
[ksnap] Snapshotting key datasets (manual-20260929-230556)
[ksnap]   rpool/ROOT@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap]   rpool/ROOT/onyx@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap]   rpool/home@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap]   rpool/home/anthony@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap]   rpool/srv@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap]   rpool/var/lib@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap] Done: 6 snapshot(s).
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss One directory, and back again (path, rollback)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323
[ksnap] Snapshotting rpool/ksnaptest-3296323
[ksnap] Done: rpool/ksnaptest-3296323@manual-20260929-230548
# (exited 0)
$ cat /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323/notes.txt
second
# (exited 0)
$ ksnap rollback /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323
[ksnap] Rolling back rpool/ksnaptest-3296323 to rpool/ksnaptest-3296323@manual-20260929-230548
[ksnap] Done. /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323 is now at: rpool/ksnaptest-3296323@manual-20260929-230548
# (exited 0)
$ cat /var/tmp/ksnaptest-3296323/notes.txt
first
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
The file had read
.Ql first
when the snapshot was taken and was changed to
.Ql second
after.
.Ss Everything there is (list)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap list

  SNAPSHOT                                           CREATED                USED
  --------                                           -------                ----
  rpool/ROOT@manual-20260929-230556                  Tue Sep 29 23:05 2026  0B
  rpool/ROOT/onyx@manual-20260929-230556             Tue Sep 29 23:05 2026  148K
\&...

  2130 snapshots total

# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Removing one (destroy)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap destroy rpool/ROOT@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap] Destroying snapshot: rpool/ROOT@manual-20260929-230556
[ksnap] Done.
# (exited 0)
$ ksnap destroy rpool/home@nosuch-snapshot
[ksnap] Destroying snapshot: rpool/home@nosuch-snapshot
could not find any snapshots to destroy; check snapshot names.
[ksnap] could not destroy rpool/home@nosuch-snapshot (above)
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap rollback /
[ksnap] rpool/ROOT/onyx is the running root filesystem; roll it back with kldload-rollback (boot environments), not here
# (exited 1)
$ ksnap rollback /dev/shm
[ksnap] /dev/shm is not on a ZFS dataset
# (exited 1)
$ ksnap rollback
[ksnap] rollback needs a path: ksnap rollback /path
# (exited 2)
$ ksnap frob
[ksnap] unknown command: frob
Usage: ksnap [list|rollback <path>|destroy <snap>|<path>]
\&...
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Ss Usage (-h, --help)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ksnap --help
Usage: ksnap [list|rollback <path>|destroy <snap>|<path>]

  ksnap              Snapshot all key datasets
  ksnap /home/admin  Snapshot a specific path
  ksnap list         Show all snapshots
  ksnap rollback /x  Roll back to last snapshot
  ksnap destroy NAME Destroy a snapshot

A path is resolved to the ZFS dataset holding it; a path that is not on
ZFS is refused. The root filesystem is never rolled back here: use
kldload-rollback, which boots an earlier boot environment instead.

Exit: 0 done, 1 a snapshot or rollback failed, 2 usage error.
See ksnap(8).
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "[ksnap] PATH is not on a ZFS dataset"
The path is on another filesystem, or does not exist.
.It "[ksnap] No snapshots found for DATASET"
Nothing to roll back to.
.It "[ksnap] DATASET is the running root filesystem; roll it back with kldload-rollback (boot environments), not here"
See
.Xr kldload-rollback 8 .
.It "[ksnap] zfs refused the rollback (above); PATH is unchanged"
.It "[ksnap] could not destroy SNAPSHOT (above)"
.It "[ksnap] could not snapshot DATASET (above)"
ZFS's own reason is the line before.
.It "[ksnap] N taken, M failed"
Some of the machine's datasets were not snapshotted; each is named above.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kldload-rollback 8 ,
.Xr kldload-vm-snapshot 8 ,
.Xr kvm-snap 8 ,
.Xr sanoid 8 ,
.Xr zfs-rollback 8 ,
.Xr zfs-snapshot 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: a run with no argument ignored every
snapshot that failed and reported success;
.Ql ksnap rollback /
would have rolled back the running system under itself; and a path that
was not on ZFS was taken for a dataset named after its filesystem, such as
.Ql tmpfs .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
.Cm rollback
goes to the newest snapshot of the dataset, which is often one taken on a
schedule by
.Xr sanoid 8
rather than one taken by
.Nm ;
check with
.Cm list
first.
To go further back, use
.Ql zfs rollback -r ,
which destroys every snapshot newer than the one named.
.Pp
Rolling back a mounted filesystem changes its files under any program that
has them open.
.Pp
Snapshots are not recursive: a dataset below one of those named, such as
a machine's disk under
.Pa rpool/vms ,
is not included; that is
.Xr kvm-snap 8 .
